model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A fleet row whose count no stream can lower, because its releases were lost with an object's isolate, never retires by itself. The signed fleet-retire command, sent by the fleet lane's retire-row mode under a coordinator GO and naming the row by its key, ends it. It refuses unless the key names exactly one row, that row is draining, never ready, and its count stood still for the leaked-streams bound of 300 s in the controller's ledger. The cell then refuses too unless its own count is draining and known, and, while it isn't already 0, stood still as long and holds no lease its current object instance took by the lease stamps, since such a stream may still be live. The leases it can end are the ones the proof release ends when the 8317 read finds no connection: unstamped leases and model sockets another instance took. A stale lease normally clears by that proof by itself; the command is for when it doesn't, because the read keeps finding a connection or fails. It reads its established connections on 8317 as evidence only, releases the still count, honours the caller's abort before committing, and logs a closed POOL_RUNTIME_STILL_RELEASED line. Once the cell has answered, the controller lowers the ledger to 0 only while the ledger is still what it read, otherwise upkeep's reconcile lowers it, and logs one closed POOL_FLEET_ROW_RETIRE_OVERRIDE line whether or not the ledger step succeeded, naming the GO, the row key, both overridden counts with how long each stood, whether the ledger was lowered, and the connection reading. Upkeep's ordinary retirement then stops the cell and holds its place until the provider proves the instance gone. A model socket the cell passes through natively can outlive the isolate that took its lease, so it can still be cut; the 8317 reading is the evidence that none is open, the lane warns when it isn't 0, and the command takes a GO and acts only on a draining row. Before it, the read-only fleet-retire-check command, sent by the lane's retire-check mode with any go text, runs the same refusal tests through one shared rule check on the cell, reads the same 8317 connections and says by the stamps how many of the cell's leases its current instance holds as calls and as model sockets, how many sockets another instance took, how many are unstamped, and any count with no lease record, writing nothing anywhere. The proof release, the override and the check share one 8317 read. The lane's configure-and-accept takes an optional target-active within the configuration's 1 to 100,000, refused before anything is sent when the run's GO already configured another one, and its inspect prints the permission's revision, targetActive and maximum."
    }
  }
}
